[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r11
diagnosis_text:
There is no network “split” between 20.x (OSPF side) and 120.x (EIGRP side): end-to-end reachability is intact. However, one half of the two‑way redistribution at the OSPF–EIGRP boundary is missing. The affected component is FRR EIGRP on r11 (the boundary router), specifically the absence of OSPF-to-EIGRP redistribution.

Mechanism: OSPF prefixes are not being injected into EIGRP, so EIGRP routers (e.g., r12) do not learn 20.0.0.0/8. Despite that, traffic still flows from 120.x hosts to 20.x via a kernel default route on r12 pointing to r11, and the reverse direction works because r11 does redistribute EIGRP into OSPF.

Localization and evidence:
- Connectivity: pc12 (120.1.1.10) ↔ pc0 (20.1.1.10) pings succeed both ways (0% loss).
- OSPF side learns EIGRP: on r1, “show ip route 120.0.0.0/8” shows OSPF route via 111.1.1.2 (r11).
- EIGRP side does NOT learn OSPF: on r12, “show ip route 20.0.0.0/8” returns “Network not in table.”
- r11 FRR config: router ospf has “redistribute eigrp”, but router eigrp 100 lacks “redistribute ospf”.

Root cause: Missing “redistribute ospf” under EIGRP on r11 after the maintenance touch.

Corrective fix: On r11, under “router eigrp 100”, add OSPF redistribution with a proper metric, e.g.:
- eigrp default-metric 10000 100 255 1 1500
- redistribute ospf
This will restore two‑way redistribution; no other devices require changes.
=== END ===